+
+  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer on career options after BSc</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    A three-year BSc, on its own, is an entry ticket, not a finished career. The doors above entry level need either a
+    master's degree, a four-year degree with high marks, or a specific skill that companies pay for.
+  </p>
+
+  <p>
+    That is why the questions students really type are not "what are BSc career options" but "which combination in BSc has more scope",
+    "what can I do after BSc if I do not want to do MSc" and "is a BSc still worth it or worthless". Each of these has a
+    narrow answer once you look at subjects, exams and money together.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
+    <p><strong>The big picture in numbers.</strong> The Periodic Labour Force Survey 2025 put unemployment among graduates at about 11.2%, against
+    a national rate of 3.1%, as reported by Forbes India. India Skills Report 2026 put employability of science graduates at 61%, against 70% for engineering and 56% for
+    all graduates. A BSc is neither hopeless nor safe. What separates outcomes is what you add to it.</p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2 id="subject-gate">Why your BSc subjects decide the doors</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    Most competing pages list the same ten courses for every BSc graduate. In practice, a physics-maths-chemistry graduate and a botany-zoology-chemistry graduate
+    are standing in front of different gates. Here is how the subject groups differ.
+  </p>
+
