+
+  <h2 id="situation">Which "without maths" are you?</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    Students and parents ask this in many forms. Can a commerce student without maths do BBA and then an MBA? Is BBA a good option without maths? Is there less scope in commerce without maths? Parents add the sharper one: "without maths, commerce is nothing."
+  </p>
+
+  <p>
+    That last line is the misconception to test. It confuses three different things: a subject on the marksheet, a section in an entrance exam, and the daily maths of a job. Each has a different answer.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="callout callout--amber">
+    <p class="callout__eyebrow">The usual bad advice</p>
+    <ul class="callout__list">
+      <li>"No maths means no CA, no BBA, no MBA." Wrong on all three, as the tables below show.</li>
+      <li>"Take maths anyway, just in case." Fine if you can sustain it, costly if it damages your marks and confidence.</li>
+      <li>"Websites list what you can do, so trust the list." The popular lists contradict each other and sometimes the official documents.</li>
+    </ul>
+    <p class="callout__footer">
+      The only advice worth acting on is the one you can confirm on the college or exam page yourself.
+    </p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2 id="accountancy">Accountancy is not maths, and the boring 80% of commerce work agrees</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    Class 11 commerce without maths still has Accountancy, Business Studies and Economics as its core. Accountancy is arithmetic with rules: journal entries, ledgers, balance sheets. If you can be careful and consistent, you can do it.
+  </p>
+
+  <p>
+    A typical week in a commerce job without formal maths looks like this. An accounts assistant reconciles bank entries and files GST returns. A marketing executive reads a dashboard and reports which ad group cost less per lead. A sales executive follows up leads and updates a tracker. None of this needs calculus. It needs accuracy, spreadsheets and clear writing.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="honest-take">
+    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
+    <p>
+      The maths you cannot avoid is the school-level kind: percentages, ratios, interest, averages and reading a table. Fear of that layer causes more career damage than the missing subject does.
+    </p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2 id="applied-maths">The Applied Maths middle path most families miss</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    CBSE introduced Applied Mathematics for Classes 11 and 12 from the 2020-21 session, because standard Mathematics suits science subjects better than commerce or social-science subjects. It targets practical maths for business, finance and economics. CBSE's own advice, as reported, is that students heading for Mathematics Honours or engineering should take standard Mathematics.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="comparison-grid">
+    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
+      <div class="compare-card__label">Applied Mathematics can suit you if</div>
+      <ul>
+        <li>You want DU's BMS, BBA (FIA) or Economics Honours to stay possible, since these accept "Mathematics/Applied Mathematics".</li>
+        <li>You are comfortable with practical, business-style problems but not pure maths.</li>
+        <li>You are still in Class 10 or the start of Class 11.</li>
+      </ul>
+    </div>
+    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
+      <div class="compare-card__label">Skip it if</div>
+      <ul>
+        <li>Your marks or confidence in maths are already under strain and you have solid alternatives.</li>
+        <li>You are targeting CA, law, marketing, HR or accounting practice, where it is not a gate.</li>
+        <li>You would rather use the time to build a skill.</li>
+      </ul>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+
+  <p class="note-line">
+    Always confirm that your target college accepts Applied Mathematics in the same line as Mathematics. Some do, and the DU 2026-27 bulletin writes it as "Mathematics/Applied Mathematics".
+  </p>
+
