+  <h2 id="money-check">The money check before you sign a fee form</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    Use a strict planning heuristic: if the full education budget is Rs 10 lakh, about Rs 1 lakh is a
+    conservative starting benchmark for college fees, and spending above roughly 20% is a serious caution point that needs course
+    specific evidence. This is a heuristic, not a law. Most MBA fees will cross it, so the burden of proof
+    rises: verified median placement, your lane's recruiters on campus, and a payback plan that survives a
+    weak first year.
+  </p>
+
+  <ul>
+    <li>Ask for the median package, the share of the batch that opted for placement, and the role list by specialization.</li>
+    <li>Talk to two recent alumni in the lane you want, not the ones the college introduces.</li>
+    <li>Compute payback after tax and after loan repayments, and keep an emergency buffer.</li>
+    <li>Compare with a work-first route: a good job now, a certification, and a part-time or executive programme later. See <a href="/blog/college-degrees/is-executive-mba-worth-it-in-india/">executive MBA</a>, <a href="/blog/college-degrees/distance-mba-worth-it-india/">distance MBA</a>, and <a href="/blog/college-degrees/1-year-mba-india-worth-it/">1-year MBA</a>.</li>
+    <li>If you are a fresher, read <a href="/blog/college-degrees/mba-without-work-experience-india/">MBA without work experience</a>; if you are a commerce graduate, read <a href="/blog/career-options/mba-after-bcom-worth-it-in-india/">MBA after BCom</a>.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h2 id="first-test">A short test before you commit</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    Some people need a few weeks for this and some need a couple of months. Both are fine.
