+
+  <h2 id="not-a-career">PCB is a starting key, not a career</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    Students ask questions like "what if NEET does not work out?", "is there anything besides MBBS?" and "which field has good scope and pay without NEET?". These are the same questions that keep appearing in student Q&amp;A on Careers360 and Quora. They all come from one hidden assumption: that PCB points at a single door.
+  </p>
+
+  <p>
+    It does not. PCB is a subject combination that qualifies you for some gates and does not qualify you for others. Knowing both lists is the first useful step.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="comparison-grid">
+    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
+      <div class="compare-card__label">What PCB unlocks</div>
+      <ul>
+        {unlocks.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
+      </ul>
+    </div>
+    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
+      <div class="compare-card__label">What PCB alone does not unlock</div>
+      <ul>
+        {closes.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
+      </ul>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+
+  <div class="callout callout--amber">
+    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Three ideas to drop early</p>
+    <ul class="callout__list">
+      <li>"PCB means doctor or disappointment." False and damaging. Medicine is one lane, and a respected title is not the same as the right daily work.</li>
+      <li>"No NEET means no health career." Partly true in 2026 for some states and courses, and it is changing fast. See the section on why the no-NEET map is narrowing.</li>
+      <li>"Biology students cannot work in technology." Health-tech and bioinformatics reward exactly your domain knowledge, provided you build a quantitative floor.</li>
+    </ul>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2 id="neet-maths">The NEET seat maths for 2026</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    Look at the numbers once, calmly, because they explain why planning only around one exam is risky.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="stat-grid">
+    {arithmeticStats.map((item) => (
+      <div class="stat-card">
+        <div class="stat-card__num">{item.num}</div>
+        <p>{item.label}</p>
+      </div>
+    ))}
+  </div>
+
+  <p>
+    Divide the two big figures and you get roughly one MBBS seat for every 14 candidates who sat the paper. Among the 11.21 lakh who qualified, that is about eight qualified candidates for each MBBS seat. Qualifying also feeds BDS, AYUSH, veterinary and, in some states, nursing counselling, so the pressure is even wider than the MBBS count shows.
+  </p>
+
+  <p>
+    Private seats are just over half of the revised MBBS matrix. For students in Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, the same matrix lists 7,615 and 10,500 MBBS seats respectively, excluding national institutes such as AIIMS and JIPMER.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="honest-take">
+    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
+    <p>
+      Competition in medicine comes at two separate stages. NEET UG decides who gets into MBBS or BDS. A separate, equally hard NEET PG decides who gets a specialist seat years later, and specialist status is what usually drives income. A path can clear the first filter and still face the second.
+    </p>
+    <p>
+      The seat matrix is described as tentative and subject to revision, and counselling rounds add seats, so check the current counselling notice on the MCC site for your round.
+    </p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2 id="eight-lanes">PCB career options, sorted into eight lanes</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    These lanes are ordered by realistic growth headroom, meaning how far income and independence can stretch with skill and proof, not by prestige or exam difficulty. That is a judgement, not a rule. Lane 1 leads because the skill travels across employers and can be sold remotely. Medicine sits third because its ceiling is very high but reaching it needs two competitive stages and a long runway.
+  </p>
+
