+
+  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    If you are looking for certificate courses for BCom students, do not start with a list of "top 10".
+    Start with a question: <strong>who will vouch for this certificate if a recruiter phones them?</strong>
+    NISM will vouch for its own exam. Tally Education will vouch for its own course. Your local institute
+    will vouch only for itself.
+  </p>
+
+  <p>
+    Students ask the same things again and again: which course goes with BCom, is Tally with GST enough,
+    is SAP worth it, what can I do beside my degree. The honest answer is that the subject matters less
+    than the issuer and the proof you build. Advertised fees for the same subject vary so much that a student can
+    easily pay several times more for a paper that a Rs 1,500 exam or a maker's own certificate would have beaten.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="callout callout--amber">
+    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Common advice that misleads</p>
+    <ul class="callout__list">
+      <li>"Do any computer course, it looks good on the resume."</li>
+      <li>"Tally with GST alone gets a job."</li>
+      <li>"The more certificates, the better."</li>
+      <li>"Placement guaranteed, so the fee is worth it."</li>
+    </ul>
+    <p class="callout__footer">
+      Employers filter on what you can do in a test task. A certificate helps only when it is verifiable
+      and when you can show one thing you made with it.
+    </p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2 id="ladder">The 4-Rung Certificate Ladder</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    <strong>The 4-Rung Certificate Ladder</strong> is a simple way to judge any certificate before you pay.
+    Higher rung means more weight with an employer and usually a lower fee. Use it every time.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="feature-grid">
