+
+  <h2 id="verify">How to check a certificate before you pay</h2>
+
+  <div class="comparison-grid">
+    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
+      <div class="compare-card__label">Good signs</div>
+      <ul>
+        <li>The certificate comes from the tool-maker, exam body, or a university-run portal.</li>
+        <li>An employer can verify it online.</li>
+        <li>The course ends in something you build.</li>
+        <li>Refund terms are written before you pay.</li>
+        <li>You can speak to two past students.</li>
+      </ul>
+    </div>
+    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
+      <div class="compare-card__label">Warning signs</div>
+      <ul>
+        <li>"Guaranteed job" or a fixed salary promise.</li>
+        <li>Fee jumps if you do not decide today.</li>
+        <li>The syllabus lists tools but no project.</li>
+        <li>The certificate name copies a famous brand but the issuer is the institute.</li>
+        <li>They will not name any past student.</li>
+      </ul>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2 id="gates">Pass The 3 Gates before you pay for any certificate</h2>
+
+  <div class="gates-block">
+    <p class="gates-block__title">Three real-world tests. Skip a certificate if you cannot pass all three.</p>
+    <div class="gates-row">
+      <div class="gate">
+        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
+        <span class="gate__name">Proof of skill</span>
+        <p>Try the free version first. Watch a current YouTube tutorial series from a working accountant, do a free Microsoft Learn module, one NISM study chapter, or one SWAYAM lecture. Finish a small task with it before you spend money.</p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="gate">
+        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
+        <span class="gate__name">Proof of communication</span>
+        <p>Say in under two minutes which job this certificate is for and why that job needs it. If your answer is only "it looks good", wait.</p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="gate">
+        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
+        <span class="gate__name">Proof of value</span>
+        <p>Speak to three people doing that job, such as an accounts executive, a broking associate, or a reporting analyst. Ask if the certificate mattered in their hiring or if a test task did.</p>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2 id="not-enough">When a certificate is the wrong tool</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    Some goals need a qualification, not a certificate. Statutory audit in India needs CA. Company
+    secretary work needs CS. A certificate cannot replace them. If a professional route is your goal, the
+    honest advice is in the guide to
+    <a href="/blog/college-degrees/best-courses-after-bcom-for-high-salary/">best courses after BCom for high salary</a>.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
+    <p>
+      <strong>Check your budget.</strong> As a strict planning guideline, not a law, try to keep the
+      spend on any single certificate near 10% of your yearly education budget. On Rs 1,00,000 a year,
+      that is about Rs 10,000, which covers several NISM exams and free courses with room left. Going
+      well above that needs a specific reason, such as a credential your target job requires.
+    </p>
+  </div>
+
+  <p>
+    Government exams like bank PO and SSC are another path. Certificates add little to them, so keep any
+    certificate spend small and build a parallel skill in case one attempt does not work out.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2 id="ai">What AI changes for BCom certificates</h2>
+
+  <p>
+    AI tools already help with formula writing, first-draft reconciliations, and dashboard layouts. That
+    lowers the value of a certificate that only tests memory of button clicks. It raises the value of
+    judgment: spotting a wrong figure, explaining a variance, and checking what the tool produced. Learn
+    one workflow where AI speeds up your accounts or analysis work, and keep a record of how you checked
+    its output. Verified human judgment is the layer that stays useful.
+  </p>
